<div class="bg-zinc-950 text-zinc-100">
  <.marketing_nav signed_in?={@signed_in?} current={:docs} />

  <section class="py-12 sm:py-16">
    <.docs_layout
      current="teams-and-access"
      updated="October 4, 2026"
      toc={[
        {"roles", "Roles"},
        {"inviting", "Inviting your team"},
        {"service-accounts", "Service accounts"},
        {"profiles", "Member profiles"},
        {"runner-scopes", "Per-member scope: runners and packs"},
        {"mfa", "MFA"},
        {"sessions", "Sessions & credentials"}
      ]}
    >
      <.docs_header current="teams-and-access">
        <:dek>
          Roles control what members can do. Runner and pack access limits where they can act.
          An API key inherits its owner's action access.
        </:dek>
      </.docs_header>

      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/screenshots/team-page.webp"
        title="emisar console · Team"
        caption="The member roster shows roles and access, with the account's security settings beside it."
        alt="The desktop Team page with member filters, Viewer and Operator rows showing runner and pack access, and the security settings alongside the roster."
      />

      <.docs_h2 id="roles">Roles</.docs_h2>
      <div class="mt-5 overflow-hidden rounded-xl border border-zinc-800">
        <table class="w-full text-left text-sm">
          <thead class="bg-zinc-900/60 text-xs uppercase tracking-wide text-zinc-400">
            <tr>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Role</th>
              <th class="px-4 py-3">Can do</th>
            </tr>
          </thead>
          <tbody class="divide-y divide-zinc-900 text-zinc-300">
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Owner</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Everything an admin can do, plus managing other owners and access to every
                runner and pack. Owner access cannot be narrowed. The last owner cannot
                be removed or demoted, so an account always has an owner.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Admin</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                The team, policies, runbooks (deleting them included), runners, pack trust,
                API keys, runner scopes, and billing (plan, card, invoices), plus everything
                the roles below can do.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Billing manager</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                The subscription, payment method, and invoices, plus reading the member list
                and billing audit events, and nothing else (no runners, policies, runbooks,
                approvals, or actions). This lets procurement own billing without touching
                infrastructure.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Operator</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Dispatch actions, <strong class="text-zinc-200">decide approvals</strong>,
                and mint their own agent keys. Within their runner and pack access they create,
                edit, publish, and discard runbooks. Deleting one takes an admin. They can
                view runners, runs, policies, and audit records, and they cannot manage the
                team, policies, or trust.
              </td>
            </tr>
            <tr>
              <td class="px-4 py-3 font-semibold text-zinc-100">Viewer</td>
              <td class="px-4 py-3">
                Read runners, runs, approvals, runbooks, policies, and audit records. They
                cannot dispatch or decide anything.
              </td>
            </tr>
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <.docs_h2 id="inviting">Inviting your team</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Open <a
          href={~p"/app/team/invite"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Team → Invite</a>, enter an email, choose a role, runner access, and pack access, and
        send the invitation. The invited person opens the link, enters their name, and confirms a
        code sent to the invited address; that accepts the invitation and signs them in to this
        workspace. When the workspace requires SSO, they then sign in at the identity provider,
        which links that identity to the new member. The workspace's MFA requirement still
        applies. Only an owner can grant the owner role
        or manage another owner. Admins can invite and manage members up to admin.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        An invitation stays bound to the address it was sent to, and only that mailbox can accept
        it. Resending replaces its old link. If emisar says
        the invitation can no longer be resent, remove that pending invitation and send a new
        one to the address you intend to invite.
      </p>

      <.docs_screenshot
        src="/images/screenshots/team-invite.webp"
        title="emisar console · Team → Invite"
        caption="Each role carries a one-line description of what it can do, so the choice is made in the form."
        alt="The invite form: an email field; the role choices: owner, admin, billing manager, operator (selected), and viewer, each with a one-line description of what it can do; an Access section offering no runners (selected), all runners, or selected runners; and a Send invite button."
        preview_h="max-h-[32rem]"
      />

      <.docs_h2 id="service-accounts">Service accounts</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A service account is a member that an app connects as, such as a team bot. Use one when a
        connection should not belong to a person: it keeps working when people leave, and the
        audit log attributes its requests to the service account.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Add one from <a
          href={~p"/app/service-accounts"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Settings → Service accounts</a>. It has the Operator role and starts with your runner and
        pack access; narrow it with Actions → Edit access like any member's. It has no email
        address and cannot sign in, so it is never invited, never enrolls MFA, and never links an
        SSO identity. Only owners and admins add and manage service accounts. Giving one access,
        connecting an app as it, or creating its keys also needs runner and pack access that
        covers the service account's.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">An app that signs in with OAuth.</strong>
            When the app asks you to authorize it, choose the service account under Connect as,
            or create a new one named after the app.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            <strong class="text-zinc-200">An app that takes an API key.</strong>
            Choose Actions → Create API key in the service account's row. Give the app the key
            and the server URL that page shows.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        emisar records who connected each app or created each key for a service account. When a
        policy doesn't allow self-approval, that person can't approve the requests the service
        account makes through that app or key. Another approver can, or an owner or admin can
        override with a reason the audit log records. Suspending or removing a service account
        revokes every key and connection that acts as it.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="profiles">Member profiles</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A member's display name and email address belong to this workspace. They identify the
        person in the team, activity and audit views, and the address receives workspace
        notifications and sign-in codes. The address starts as the one the person was invited
        at or signed up with, or the one your identity provider supplied.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Edit your display name from Profile, or a teammate's from Team if your role allows it.
        Directory-managed names are read-only here; update them in the identity provider.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A member who signs in with an emailed code can change their own email from Profile.
        They confirm it's them with their authenticator or a code sent to the current address,
        then enter a code sent to the new address. The old address gets a notice. An address
        that came from your identity provider changes when its directory sync sends a new one,
        and admins can't change another member's email.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="runner-scopes">Per-member scope: runners and packs</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Runner and pack grants limit action permissions for admins, operators, and viewers.
        Set access from Actions → Edit access on <a
          href={~p"/app/team"}
          class="font-medium text-brand-300 underline decoration-brand-500/30 underline-offset-4 hover:text-brand-200"
        >Team</a>, or map SSO/SCIM directory groups. Owners always have account-wide access;
        billing managers have no runner or pack access.
      </p>
      <ul class="mt-4 space-y-3 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            A <strong class="text-zinc-200">group scope</strong>
            follows a runner group, such as <.docs_inline_code>group: cassandra-prod</.docs_inline_code>. New
            runners in that group enter scope automatically.
          </span>
        </li>
        <li class="flex items-start gap-2.5">
          <span class="flex-none text-zinc-500">—</span>
          <span>
            A <strong class="text-zinc-200">runner scope</strong>
            grants action access to one specific
            host. Use it for a contractor who needs exactly one machine.
          </span>
        </li>
      </ul>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Each grant also has a pack scope: all packs by default, or an explicit list.
        This lets a DBA run
        <.docs_inline_code>postgres.*</.docs_inline_code>
        on database hosts without gaining
        <.docs_inline_code>shell</.docs_inline_code>
        access. A runner group alone cannot express that, because restricted packs exist on
        most hosts. The two dimensions combine: a member can act only on runners in their runner
        scope, using packs in their pack scope.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Members with access to operational pages can see the workspace's runners, packs,
        runs, output, approvals, and runbooks. Runner and pack scope limits actions, not what
        they can view.
        An API key inherits its owner's scope, so narrowing a member to
        <.docs_inline_code>cassandra-*</.docs_inline_code>
        narrows their agent with it, with no per-key settings to update. Every scope change is
        audited.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        An admin scoped to one group cannot act on the rest of the fleet. Owners are exempt
        from runner and pack scoping, but not from policy, required approvals, pack trust,
        suspension, or the account boundary. Nobody can edit their own access.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Promoting a member to Owner grants all runners and packs. Their connected agents
        inherit the wider access without reconnecting.
        Demoting an Owner requires choosing their new access. For a directory-managed Owner,
        choose Use directory role: directory sync sets both role and access, with Viewer
        privileges and no runner or pack access until it completes.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="mfa">MFA</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        <strong class="text-zinc-200">Require MFA</strong>
        requires each session to prove the member's active factor before it can use the
        workspace. Members can use an authenticator app or a sign-in through an SSO connection
        configured to satisfy MFA. The Team page's Authenticator count shows local enrollment,
        not whether every member satisfies the account's MFA requirement. Factor setup and
        the SSO exemption are in <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/authentication" <> "#enforcement"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Enforce account sign-in</.link>.
      </p>
      <p class="mt-4 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        A member's authenticator belongs to that member in this workspace; someone in two
        workspaces sets up each one separately. An owner or admin can reset a teammate's
        authenticator from the Team page if their role allows it, after entering their own
        authenticator or recovery code, or signing in again through an SSO connection that
        satisfies MFA. Emisar support can also reset it. A reset ends every session the member
        has in this workspace.
      </p>

      <.docs_h2 id="sessions">Sessions &amp; credentials</.docs_h2>
      <p class="mt-5 text-base leading-7 text-zinc-400">
        Suspend access blocks access to this workspace, ends the member's sessions, and
        revokes the membership's agent credentials and standing approvals.
        Restore access restores membership
        access, but does not restore those credentials or approvals. A manual suspension stays
        in place across directory sync; a directory-deactivated member must first be reactivated
        in the identity provider. Sign-in mechanics and session
        self-service are in <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/authentication"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Authentication</.link>. Key lifecycle and revocation are in <.link
          navigate={~p"/docs/agents-and-keys"}
          class="text-brand-400 hover:text-brand-300"
        >Manage agents &amp; keys</.link>.
      </p>
    </.docs_layout>
  </section>

  <.marketing_footer />
</div>
